<hr />
<p><span style="white-space: pre-wrap;">&#32;</span></p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>CLASSIFICATION NOTICE:</strong></p>
<hr />
<p>As the containment of SCP-001 requires an incomplete understanding of its purpose, parameters, and function, the Classification Committee has voted to establish the <strong>Atelis</strong> object class. Atelis denotes an object that requires the intentional ignorance of related matters, including secondary and tertiary concerns, in order to be contained. Any objects or personnel separated by two degrees or fewer from an Atelis-class object are to be flagged and processed in compliance with established protocols.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><span style="white-space: pre-wrap;">&#32;</span><br />
<strong>Special Containment Procedures:</strong> Secure Area-00 has been established around the space believed to contain SCP-001 and classified Level 6/Cosmic Secret. The coordinates of Secure Area-00 are asymmetrically stored in the cranial implants of 15 selected members of Foundation Leadership, considered from a pool of Ethics Committee members and Site Directors. Data contained within these implants may only be decrypted by a super-majority vote (80%). Candidates are unaware of their status as voters until and unless a vote is called.</p>
<p>In the event that the cipher becomes compromised or a cranial implant fails to ping, COGITO.aic will generate a new cryptographic protocol and distribute it to a new group of candidates. After the protocol has been updated, COGITO.aic will self-delete in order to purge the knowledge of Secure Area-00 and a new 'AIC' will be cloned from the template and promoted.</p>
<p>No access to Secure Area-00 is permitted at any time for any reason. All relevant site operation protocols, internal and external defenses, schematics, contingencies, and other related information have been expunged.</p>
<p>Per the constraints of the <span style="color: red"><strong>Atelis</strong></span> object class, under no circumstances are Site Directors or members of the Ethics Committee allowed access to any portion of this file unless a decryption vote is taking place.</p>
<p><strong>Description:</strong> SCP-001 is the absence of the O5 Council. In spite of all historical evidence detailing the activities of the O5 Council, the identities and histories of its members, and the existence of complex support structures directly and indirectly attached to the object, the O5 Council does not definitively exist.</p>
<p>All contemporary records relating to the O5 Council - other than those contained in this file - are believed to be either fabricated by personnel under the influence of, or directly manifested by, SCP-001 itself. All data related to the orchestration of these events has been expunged in accordance with <span style="color: red"><strong>Atelis</strong></span> protocols.</p>
</div>
<hr />
<p><span style="font-size:1.5em;"><strong>Addendum 01:</strong></span><br />
<span style="font-size:1.25em;"><strong>Conclusion of the Cohen Administration</strong></span></p>
<hr />
<div style="text-align: left;">
<p>The following epistolary chain began at the end of Administrator Cohen's tenure as he formally transitioned duties and classified materials to his designated successor, H.V. Olivander. While this chain of communication was originally designated as complete, there were a number of correspondences between involved parties which may have contained additional information or context on this matter. These records have been destroyed in accordance with <span style="color: red"><strong>Atelis</strong></span> protocols.</p>
<p>This chain of communication is the earliest known reference to the O5 Council in any recorded fashion.</p>
<p>Should any additional records be discovered pertaining to this matter, personnel are instructed to immediately destroy these records and contact Internal Security using challenge response &quot;Carthage&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>FROM:</strong> Administrator Emeritus, William Cohen<br />
<strong>TO:</strong> Administrator Pro Tempore, H.V. Olivander</p>
<hr />
<p>Harold,</p>
<p>In my fifth and final missive I would speak frankly with you about the company you keep. Trust is very important and as much as any competent administrator or leader should trust themselves, they should also trust that their interests are well represented.</p>
<p>This disease that clings to my mind like tar has caused me to no longer trust myself, and so, I pass this knowledge on to you while it remains untainted.</p>
<p>I remember the day I was first invited to speak to the Council. O5-1 approached me beforehand, sat me down, and told me that they felt I was going to make an error in judgment; that my priorities were wrong. It was tough to swallow but the seriousness of their voice and the passion of their rhetoric made it clear that we were on the same side. That we wanted the same thing. I didn't know what to do but their counsel helped me find the truth through the fog. In the middle of the night, standing at the head of the table of the O5 Council, deep in the bunker of Site-00, I addressed them. If only they knew how my knees quaked the entire time I spoke.</p>
<p>After my briefing had concluded, the O5s approved of and accepted my recommendation unanimously. Ironically, I don't remember what that briefing was even about anymore, but I remember the professional satisfaction that followed. It still comes to me in my idle thoughts much like my wedding day, or the very first Anomalous Incident I responded to. I can touch that memory in the space of my mind with the same certainty as I recall my own name.</p>
<p>Yet in spite of this I must assert with equal clarity that this meeting never took place.</p>
<p>Terms like 'Overseer' and Site-00 were never more than a concept, a dream. Never brick and mortar. Yet I remember. I've been there. I've spoken to them and they've spoken to me. At this point in my life I fear what I know almost as much as I fear what I have forgotten.</p>
<p>By the time you receive this letter, my retirement will be complete. As my final professional act, I will report to Site-00 tomorrow morning to hand in my credentials and express my appreciation one last time to those who mentored me for so long.</p>
<p>The Foundation is in good hands.</p>
<p>Best wishes,<br />
William Cohen<br />
Former Administrator</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<hr />
<p><span style="font-size:1.5em;"><strong>Addendum 02:</strong></span><br />
